\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Physical force field deconvolution, multi-scale matched filters, and random matrix theory resolve complex reticulations.} \textbf  {(A)} Metric Force Field \& Hookean Restoring Potential: Vector potential landscapes and directional velocity reversal across the authentic 3,120-nucleotide HIV-1 \textit  {pol} benchmark ($N = 22$; 8 Subtype B, 8 Subtype C, 3 CRF07\_BC, 3 CRF08\_BC). In Segment 1 (Protease flank, $0\text  {--}862\text  { nt}$), the potential well $U(\mathbf  {z})$ centers on Subtype C (Home, vermilion square), exerting continuous Hookean restoring forces that retain chimeras CRF07\_BC (green diamonds) and CRF08\_BC (purple triangles) within the Subtype C basin. In Segment 2 (Reverse Transcriptase cassette, $862\text  {--}1169\text  { nt}$), the basin abruptly shifts toward Subtype B (Donor, navy blue circle). Below, projected trajectory velocity along the Subtype B/C dipole axis exhibits an instantaneous directional velocity reversal for CRF08\_BC isolate AY008715 ($\Delta v = 0.092$, $Z_{\text  {flip}} = 22.5$, $p < 10^{-15}$). Coordinates shift from Protease flank $\bar  {Z}_1 = -0.07$ to RT cassette $\bar  {Z}_1 = +0.02$. \textbf  {(B)} Multi-Scale Dyadic Filters \& Running Drawdown: Resolution of the 307-nucleotide RT cassette ($[862, 1169]\text  { nt}$) in HIV-1 \textit  {pol}. Under whole-gene normalization, the 1D Brownian bridge undergoes signal dilution: cumulative bridge excursion peaks downstream at $u = 2450\text  { nt}$, diluting the local cassette signal ($B(u)/(\sigma \sqrt  {L})$, top). Filtering trajectory flux through a dyadic wavelet bank (middle) overcomes dilution: the matched dyadic scale $\tau = 250\text  { nt}$ (amber) resonates at the cassette boundaries, producing sharp impulses at entry ($u_1 = 862$) and exit ($u_2 = 1169$). Tracking cumulative flux $Y(u)$, high-water mark $M(u)$, and running drawdown $\text  {DD}(u) = M(u) - Y(u)$ (bottom) localizes cassette entry and pinpoints the abrupt momentum collapse at cassette exit ($u_2 = 1169\text  { nt}$). \textbf  {(C)} Spatio-Temporal Force Field \& SVD Mode Peeling: Deconvolution of multi-parental mosaicism across HIV-1 \textit  {pol}. Spatial profiles (top) separate macro-clade Subtype B/C backbone divergence ($\mathbf  {u}_1$) from localized chimeric reticulations across Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), and Integrase (IN) domains ($\mathbf  {u}_2$). Taxon loadings (bottom) decouple ancestry from reticulation. Pure references stay near baseline on Mode 2 ($V_2 \approx 0$). Chimeras CRF07\_BC (green diamonds, $V_2 \approx -0.3$) and CRF08\_BC (purple triangles, $V_2 < -0.05$) project strongly into the reticulation subspace. Rank-1 mode peeling deflates each reticulation event without combinatorial searches. \textbf  {(D)} Random Matrix Theory Halting \& Continuous Topological Mode Ratio: Parameter-free stopping and topological mode classification. Singular value spectrum on HIV-1 \textit  {pol} (left) plotted against the Marchenko-Pastur bulk spectral noise edge ($s_{\text  {bulk}} = 4.62$, red dashed line). Modes 1--11 reside above $s_{\text  {bulk}}$ (SNR up to 128.5), while higher modes merge into the noise floor, terminating mode peeling objectively at Mode 12. Right: continuous spatial mode profiles classify reticulation topologies: the leading backbone mode forms a monopolar global arch ($p_{\text  {mono}} = 0.97$, blue), whereas the reticulation mode forms a localized bipolar cassette pulse returning to baseline ($p_{\text  {mono}} = 0.48$, vermilion).\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:theory_force_field}{{4}{9}{\textbf {Physical force field deconvolution, multi-scale matched filters, and random matrix theory resolve complex reticulations.} \textbf {(A)} Metric Force Field \& Hookean Restoring Potential: Vector potential landscapes and directional velocity reversal across the authentic 3,120-nucleotide HIV-1 \textit {pol} benchmark ($N = 22$; 8 Subtype B, 8 Subtype C, 3 CRF07\_BC, 3 CRF08\_BC). In Segment 1 (Protease flank, $0\text {--}862\text { nt}$), the potential well $U(\mathbf {z})$ centers on Subtype C (Home, vermilion square), exerting continuous Hookean restoring forces that retain chimeras CRF07\_BC (green diamonds) and CRF08\_BC (purple triangles) within the Subtype C basin. In Segment 2 (Reverse Transcriptase cassette, $862\text {--}1169\text { nt}$), the basin abruptly shifts toward Subtype B (Donor, navy blue circle). Below, projected trajectory velocity along the Subtype B/C dipole axis exhibits an instantaneous directional velocity reversal for CRF08\_BC isolate AY008715 ($\Delta v = 0.092$, $Z_{\text {flip}} = 22.5$, $p < 10^{-15}$). Coordinates shift from Protease flank $\bar {Z}_1 = -0.07$ to RT cassette $\bar {Z}_1 = +0.02$. \textbf {(B)} Multi-Scale Dyadic Filters \& Running Drawdown: Resolution of the 307-nucleotide RT cassette ($[862, 1169]\text { nt}$) in HIV-1 \textit {pol}. Under whole-gene normalization, the 1D Brownian bridge undergoes signal dilution: cumulative bridge excursion peaks downstream at $u = 2450\text { nt}$, diluting the local cassette signal ($B(u)/(\sigma \sqrt {L})$, top). Filtering trajectory flux through a dyadic wavelet bank (middle) overcomes dilution: the matched dyadic scale $\tau = 250\text { nt}$ (amber) resonates at the cassette boundaries, producing sharp impulses at entry ($u_1 = 862$) and exit ($u_2 = 1169$). Tracking cumulative flux $Y(u)$, high-water mark $M(u)$, and running drawdown $\text {DD}(u) = M(u) - Y(u)$ (bottom) localizes cassette entry and pinpoints the abrupt momentum collapse at cassette exit ($u_2 = 1169\text { nt}$). \textbf {(C)} Spatio-Temporal Force Field \& SVD Mode Peeling: Deconvolution of multi-parental mosaicism across HIV-1 \textit {pol}. Spatial profiles (top) separate macro-clade Subtype B/C backbone divergence ($\mathbf {u}_1$) from localized chimeric reticulations across Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT), and Integrase (IN) domains ($\mathbf {u}_2$). Taxon loadings (bottom) decouple ancestry from reticulation. Pure references stay near baseline on Mode 2 ($V_2 \approx 0$). Chimeras CRF07\_BC (green diamonds, $V_2 \approx -0.3$) and CRF08\_BC (purple triangles, $V_2 < -0.05$) project strongly into the reticulation subspace. Rank-1 mode peeling deflates each reticulation event without combinatorial searches. \textbf {(D)} Random Matrix Theory Halting \& Continuous Topological Mode Ratio: Parameter-free stopping and topological mode classification. Singular value spectrum on HIV-1 \textit {pol} (left) plotted against the Marchenko-Pastur bulk spectral noise edge ($s_{\text {bulk}} = 4.62$, red dashed line). Modes 1--11 reside above $s_{\text {bulk}}$ (SNR up to 128.5), while higher modes merge into the noise floor, terminating mode peeling objectively at Mode 12. Right: continuous spatial mode profiles classify reticulation topologies: the leading backbone mode forms a monopolar global arch ($p_{\text {mono}} = 0.97$, blue), whereas the reticulation mode forms a localized bipolar cassette pulse returning to baseline ($p_{\text {mono}} = 0.48$, vermilion).\relax }{figure.caption.5}{}}
