\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Empirical operational phase envelope and information-theoretic observability horizon of \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  across 150,000 independent simulation trials ($N = 4\text  {--}250$ taxa, $L = 1{,}000\text  {--}30{,}000$ nt). Panel\nobreakspace  {}A: Continuous empirical power phase diagram in $(d_{\mathrm  {parent}} \times L_{\mathrm  {tract}}/L)$ parameter space. Detection follows the hyperbolic informational boundary $M_{\mathrm  {tract}} = d_{\mathrm  {parent}} \cdot L_{\mathrm  {tract}} = \text  {const}$, separating the unobservable information horizon ($M < 5$ SNPs, dark navy) from the saturation regime ($M > 25$ SNPs, bright yellow, power $> 80\%$). Panel\nobreakspace  {}B: Alignment-level false positive rate across non-recombinant nulls ($N = 75{,}000$) stratified by evolutionary rate variation $\alpha $. Strict molecular clock ($\alpha \ge 50$, $0.160\%$) and extreme Gamma heterotachy ($\alpha \le 0.5$, $0.500\%$) maintain strict false positive rate control ($<0.50\%$). Panel\nobreakspace  {}C: Breakpoint spatial resolution error $|\hat  {b} - b_{\mathrm  {true}}|$ as a function of tract mutations $M_{\mathrm  {tract}}$. At $M \ge 25$ SNPs, median spatial error stabilizes at $\le 18.0$\nobreakspace  {}nt, reaching $8.0$\nobreakspace  {}nt for $M > 100$. Panel\nobreakspace  {}D: Execution throughput and scaling across genome lengths ($L$) and cohort sizes ($N$), confirming sustained execution at $>30$\nobreakspace  {}trials per second across diverse phylogenetic regimes.\relax }}{22}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:grand_sweep_150k_phase_diagram}{{5}{22}{Empirical operational phase envelope and information-theoretic observability horizon of \RhizAeon across 150,000 independent simulation trials ($N = 4\text {--}250$ taxa, $L = 1{,}000\text {--}30{,}000$ nt). Panel~A: Continuous empirical power phase diagram in $(d_{\mathrm {parent}} \times L_{\mathrm {tract}}/L)$ parameter space. Detection follows the hyperbolic informational boundary $M_{\mathrm {tract}} = d_{\mathrm {parent}} \cdot L_{\mathrm {tract}} = \text {const}$, separating the unobservable information horizon ($M < 5$ SNPs, dark navy) from the saturation regime ($M > 25$ SNPs, bright yellow, power $> 80\%$). Panel~B: Alignment-level false positive rate across non-recombinant nulls ($N = 75{,}000$) stratified by evolutionary rate variation $\alpha $. Strict molecular clock ($\alpha \ge 50$, $0.160\%$) and extreme Gamma heterotachy ($\alpha \le 0.5$, $0.500\%$) maintain strict false positive rate control ($<0.50\%$). Panel~C: Breakpoint spatial resolution error $|\hat {b} - b_{\mathrm {true}}|$ as a function of tract mutations $M_{\mathrm {tract}}$. At $M \ge 25$ SNPs, median spatial error stabilizes at $\le 18.0$~nt, reaching $8.0$~nt for $M > 100$. Panel~D: Execution throughput and scaling across genome lengths ($L$) and cohort sizes ($N$), confirming sustained execution at $>30$~trials per second across diverse phylogenetic regimes.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Continuous recombination fields and reticulate mosaic architecture across the 30.9\nobreakspace  {}kb Sarbecovirus genome. Panel\nobreakspace  {}A: Continuous Recombination Breakpoint Flux $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across 78 genomes (Lytras et al.\ \cite  {lytras2022exploring}) and 83 genomes (+Laos BANAL bat sarbecoviruses \cite  {temmam2022bat}). An aligned gene model track demarcates ORF1ab, RdRp, Spike, ORF3a, E, M, ORF6, ORF7a, ORF8, and N. Callouts identify localized physical flux hotspots at the RdRp exit (12,867\nobreakspace  {}bp), Spike 5' NTD (21,825\nobreakspace  {}bp), codon 313 NTD/RBD hinge (22,764\nobreakspace  {}bp), Spike exit / ORF3a (25,728\nobreakspace  {}bp), and ORF8/N cluster (28,810\nobreakspace  {}bp). Panel\nobreakspace  {}B: Exact concordance with 21 published whole-genome GARD breakpoints from Lytras et al.\ Table\nobreakspace  {}S3 (median deviation 99.0\nobreakspace  {}nt, with key S1/S2 structural junctions matching within 9--12\nobreakspace  {}nt). The gray corridor indicates $\pm 150$\nobreakspace  {}nt tolerance. Panel\nobreakspace  {}C: High-resolution Spike glycoprotein mosaic trajectories (nt 21,000--26,000) across key progenitor lineages: SARS-CoV-2 (Wuhan-Hu-1), Laos bat sarbecoviruses BANAL-52 (shared ACE2-binding RBD), BANAL-116 (NTD micro-conversion and S1 introgression), BANAL-236 (PrC31 backbone with BANAL-103 RBD/S2 transfer), Yunnan bat sarbecoviruses RaTG13 (conserved NTD, divergent RBD) and RpYN06 (SARS-CoV-2 backbone with PrC31 S1 replacement), Thailand bat RacCS203, and Pangolin-CoV P5E (ACE2 RBM transfer). Panel\nobreakspace  {}D: Computational latency and scaling comparison. While exhaustive phylogenetic tree search (GARD) was computationally forced to downsample to 18 nCoV genomes (requiring 4.5 days on cluster nodes; 78 taxa estimated at $>30$ days), \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  evaluates the entire 78-taxon cohort in 58.9\nobreakspace  {}s and 83 taxa in 94.8\nobreakspace  {}s on a single CPU thread ($>10{,}000\times $ speedup).\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sarbecovirus_recombination_landscape}{{6}{23}{Continuous recombination fields and reticulate mosaic architecture across the 30.9~kb Sarbecovirus genome. Panel~A: Continuous Recombination Breakpoint Flux $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across 78 genomes (Lytras et al.\ \cite {lytras2022exploring}) and 83 genomes (+Laos BANAL bat sarbecoviruses \cite {temmam2022bat}). An aligned gene model track demarcates ORF1ab, RdRp, Spike, ORF3a, E, M, ORF6, ORF7a, ORF8, and N. Callouts identify localized physical flux hotspots at the RdRp exit (12,867~bp), Spike 5' NTD (21,825~bp), codon 313 NTD/RBD hinge (22,764~bp), Spike exit / ORF3a (25,728~bp), and ORF8/N cluster (28,810~bp). Panel~B: Exact concordance with 21 published whole-genome GARD breakpoints from Lytras et al.\ Table~S3 (median deviation 99.0~nt, with key S1/S2 structural junctions matching within 9--12~nt). The gray corridor indicates $\pm 150$~nt tolerance. Panel~C: High-resolution Spike glycoprotein mosaic trajectories (nt 21,000--26,000) across key progenitor lineages: SARS-CoV-2 (Wuhan-Hu-1), Laos bat sarbecoviruses BANAL-52 (shared ACE2-binding RBD), BANAL-116 (NTD micro-conversion and S1 introgression), BANAL-236 (PrC31 backbone with BANAL-103 RBD/S2 transfer), Yunnan bat sarbecoviruses RaTG13 (conserved NTD, divergent RBD) and RpYN06 (SARS-CoV-2 backbone with PrC31 S1 replacement), Thailand bat RacCS203, and Pangolin-CoV P5E (ACE2 RBM transfer). Panel~D: Computational latency and scaling comparison. While exhaustive phylogenetic tree search (GARD) was computationally forced to downsample to 18 nCoV genomes (requiring 4.5 days on cluster nodes; 78 taxa estimated at $>30$ days), \RhizAeon evaluates the entire 78-taxon cohort in 58.9~s and 83 taxa in 94.8~s on a single CPU thread ($>10{,}000\times $ speedup).\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
