\newlabel{subsec:spneumoniae_pmen1_reanalysis}{{{3.10}}{24}{Bacterial Pathogen Evolution: Chromosome-Scale Recombination, Vaccine Escape, and Antimicrobial Resistance in Streptococcus pneumoniae}{subsection.3.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Whole-chromosome recombination fields and micro-resolution mosaicism across the 2.22\nobreakspace  {}Mb \textit  {Streptococcus pneumoniae} PMEN1 genome ($N = 11$ taxa, $L = 2{,}221{,}315$\nobreakspace  {}bp). Panel\nobreakspace  {}A: Continuous Breakpoint Flux Density Field $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across the complete ATCC 700669 chromosome. An aligned gene model track demarcates major clinical and core loci, with callouts identifying prominent recombination flux peaks at the \textit  {pbp2x}/\textit  {cps} super-hotspot (285--352\nobreakspace  {}kb), the 732\nobreakspace  {}kb island, the 1.15\nobreakspace  {}Mb hotspot, the \textit  {folA} trimethoprim island (1.62--1.65\nobreakspace  {}Mb), the 1.72\nobreakspace  {}Mb locus, and the \textit  {pspC}/2.13\nobreakspace  {}Mb cluster. Panel\nobreakspace  {}B: Head-to-head empirical concordance against 52 published Gubbins recombination tract boundaries from Croucher et al.\ \cite  {croucher2015rapid}. Shaded corridors indicate $\pm 2.0$\nobreakspace  {}kb tolerance around the 1:1 identity line. Callouts highlight exact recovery of key clinical genes (\textit  {pbp2x}, $\Delta = 1$\nobreakspace  {}bp; \textit  {folA}, $\Delta = 19$\nobreakspace  {}bp; \textit  {pspC}, $\Delta = 1$\nobreakspace  {}bp; \textit  {cps}, $\Delta = 3.8$\nobreakspace  {}kb). 100\% of boundaries match within 5\nobreakspace  {}kb, with a median spatial deviation of only 92.5\nobreakspace  {}bp (sub-SNP resolution). Panel\nobreakspace  {}C: Micro-resolution deep dive into the 270--370\nobreakspace  {}kb AMR and vaccine escape super-hotspot. Top track shows segregating informative SNP density (SNPs/kb) with vertical dashed lines marking physical changepoints. Bottom track displays deconstructed mosaic architectures across key clinical isolates, illustrating the serotype 19A capsule switch in strain 3355 (PCV7 vaccine escape) alongside distinct mosaic \textit  {pbp2x} transpeptidase introgression tracts in strains 1731, 2682, and 1789. Panel\nobreakspace  {}D: Computational latency and scaling comparison. While ClonalFrame required 705\nobreakspace  {}hours (\nobreakspace  {}1\nobreakspace  {}month) and Gubbins required 1.5\nobreakspace  {}hours (5,400\nobreakspace  {}s), \texttt  {RhizAeon}\xspace  scans the complete 2.22\nobreakspace  {}Mb chromosome in 114\nobreakspace  {}ms in Python and 127\nobreakspace  {}ms in native Rust on a single CPU thread ($>30{,}000\times $ speedup over Gubbins, $>18{,}000{,}000\times $ over ClonalFrame).\relax }}{25}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spneumoniae_chromosome_landscape}{{7}{25}{Whole-chromosome recombination fields and micro-resolution mosaicism across the 2.22~Mb \textit {Streptococcus pneumoniae} PMEN1 genome ($N = 11$ taxa, $L = 2{,}221{,}315$~bp). Panel~A: Continuous Breakpoint Flux Density Field $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across the complete ATCC 700669 chromosome. An aligned gene model track demarcates major clinical and core loci, with callouts identifying prominent recombination flux peaks at the \textit {pbp2x}/\textit {cps} super-hotspot (285--352~kb), the 732~kb island, the 1.15~Mb hotspot, the \textit {folA} trimethoprim island (1.62--1.65~Mb), the 1.72~Mb locus, and the \textit {pspC}/2.13~Mb cluster. Panel~B: Head-to-head empirical concordance against 52 published Gubbins recombination tract boundaries from Croucher et al.\ \cite {croucher2015rapid}. Shaded corridors indicate $\pm 2.0$~kb tolerance around the 1:1 identity line. Callouts highlight exact recovery of key clinical genes (\textit {pbp2x}, $\Delta = 1$~bp; \textit {folA}, $\Delta = 19$~bp; \textit {pspC}, $\Delta = 1$~bp; \textit {cps}, $\Delta = 3.8$~kb). 100\% of boundaries match within 5~kb, with a median spatial deviation of only 92.5~bp (sub-SNP resolution). Panel~C: Micro-resolution deep dive into the 270--370~kb AMR and vaccine escape super-hotspot. Top track shows segregating informative SNP density (SNPs/kb) with vertical dashed lines marking physical changepoints. Bottom track displays deconstructed mosaic architectures across key clinical isolates, illustrating the serotype 19A capsule switch in strain 3355 (PCV7 vaccine escape) alongside distinct mosaic \textit {pbp2x} transpeptidase introgression tracts in strains 1731, 2682, and 1789. Panel~D: Computational latency and scaling comparison. While ClonalFrame required 705~hours (~1~month) and Gubbins required 1.5~hours (5,400~s), \RhizAeon scans the complete 2.22~Mb chromosome in 114~ms in Python and 127~ms in native Rust on a single CPU thread ($>30{,}000\times $ speedup over Gubbins, $>18{,}000{,}000\times $ over ClonalFrame).\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {{3.11}}Primate Immunogenomics: Resolving the 35-Year MHC Class I Domain Paradox and Trans-Species Reticulation Across Hominoidea}{25}{subsection.3.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{subsec:primate_mhc_class1}{{{3.11}}{25}{Primate Immunogenomics: Resolving the 35-Year MHC Class I Domain Paradox and Trans-Species Reticulation Across Hominoidea}{subsection.3.11}{}}
\citation{maccari2017ipd}
\citation{robinson2020ipd}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Continuous manifold deconvolution of the 35-year primate MHC Class I domain paradox across the Hominoidea superfamily ($N = 379$ taxa, $L = 1{,}128$\nobreakspace  {}bp, 526 segregating sites). Panel\nobreakspace  {}A: Continuous Breakpoint Flux Density Field $\Phi _{\mathrm  {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across the full-length coding sequence. An aligned gene model track demarcates Exons 1--7, with inside callouts identifying major kinetic inflections at the Exon\nobreakspace  {}2/3 ARS groove (268--278\nobreakspace  {}bp), the unsupervised codon 181/182 structural junction (608--615\nobreakspace  {}bp), and the Exon\nobreakspace  {}5 transmembrane anchor (971--990\nobreakspace  {}bp). Panel\nobreakspace  {}B: Structural domain distribution of 316 significant reticulate taxa ($Z \ge 3.0$). Reticulations segregate with extreme domain specificity: 93.7\% concentrate within the peptide-binding groove (Exon\nobreakspace  {}2: 48.7\%; Exon\nobreakspace  {}3: 10.8\%) and transmembrane anchor (Exon\nobreakspace  {}5: 34.2\%), while the Exon\nobreakspace  {}4 $\alpha _3$ structural stalk emerges as an evolutionary clonal coldspot (only 3.2\% of taxa). Panel\nobreakspace  {}C: Micro-resolution deconstruction of canonical primate chimeras. Top track shows the nucleotide mismatch profile of human $HLA\text  {-}B*46:01$ against its parental backbone $HLA\text  {-}B*15:01$ (0 mismatches across Exons 1, 4, 5, 6, 7) and donor $HLA\text  {-}C*01:02$ (9 diagnostic mismatches matching $C*01:02$ across the 73\nobreakspace  {}bp C1 epitope cassette in Exon\nobreakspace  {}2, localized to bp 268, $Z = 2.69$). Bottom track displays horizontal mosaic architectural ribbons across six benchmark hominoid chimeras, including chimpanzee $Patr\text  {-}AL*01:01$, gorilla $Gogo\text  {-}B*01:01$, and orangutan $Poab\text  {-}B*10:01$. Panel\nobreakspace  {}D: Side-by-side 2D Classical MDS projections resolving the domain paradox. In Exons 2--3 (ARS groove, left), balancing selection and recurrent ectopic gene conversion cause human, chimpanzee, bonobo, gorilla, and orangutan alleles to intermingle across loci ($A, B, C$). In Exon\nobreakspace  {}4 ($\alpha _3$ stalk, right), purifying selection on $\beta _2$m/CD8 binding maintains strict locus-specific monophyly, separating $A$, $B$, and $C$ into clean, unreticulated clades.\relax }}{26}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hominoidea_mhc_landscape}{{8}{26}{Continuous manifold deconvolution of the 35-year primate MHC Class I domain paradox across the Hominoidea superfamily ($N = 379$ taxa, $L = 1{,}128$~bp, 526 segregating sites). Panel~A: Continuous Breakpoint Flux Density Field $\Phi _{\mathrm {bp}}(u)$ (orange, primary axis) and Cumulative Strain $\rho (u)$ (blue, secondary axis) across the full-length coding sequence. An aligned gene model track demarcates Exons 1--7, with inside callouts identifying major kinetic inflections at the Exon~2/3 ARS groove (268--278~bp), the unsupervised codon 181/182 structural junction (608--615~bp), and the Exon~5 transmembrane anchor (971--990~bp). Panel~B: Structural domain distribution of 316 significant reticulate taxa ($Z \ge 3.0$). Reticulations segregate with extreme domain specificity: 93.7\% concentrate within the peptide-binding groove (Exon~2: 48.7\%; Exon~3: 10.8\%) and transmembrane anchor (Exon~5: 34.2\%), while the Exon~4 $\alpha _3$ structural stalk emerges as an evolutionary clonal coldspot (only 3.2\% of taxa). Panel~C: Micro-resolution deconstruction of canonical primate chimeras. Top track shows the nucleotide mismatch profile of human $HLA\text {-}B*46:01$ against its parental backbone $HLA\text {-}B*15:01$ (0 mismatches across Exons 1, 4, 5, 6, 7) and donor $HLA\text {-}C*01:02$ (9 diagnostic mismatches matching $C*01:02$ across the 73~bp C1 epitope cassette in Exon~2, localized to bp 268, $Z = 2.69$). Bottom track displays horizontal mosaic architectural ribbons across six benchmark hominoid chimeras, including chimpanzee $Patr\text {-}AL*01:01$, gorilla $Gogo\text {-}B*01:01$, and orangutan $Poab\text {-}B*10:01$. Panel~D: Side-by-side 2D Classical MDS projections resolving the domain paradox. In Exons 2--3 (ARS groove, left), balancing selection and recurrent ectopic gene conversion cause human, chimpanzee, bonobo, gorilla, and orangutan alleles to intermingle across loci ($A, B, C$). In Exon~4 ($\alpha _3$ stalk, right), purifying selection on $\beta _2$m/CD8 binding maintains strict locus-specific monophyly, separating $A$, $B$, and $C$ into clean, unreticulated clades.\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
